 "books": {
  "guide": {
   "title": "Notes on the Stair",
   "author": "Quill",
   "pages": [
    "The stair goes round the great shaft and never stops being the stair. Count the turns; the old builders cut doors where the walls are driest.\n\nFirst door, a market. Second, a drain-hall. At the bottom the water.",
    "I have walked it nine times and the water is higher every time. Not much. A hand, a foot. The sap-lamps do not care.\n\nNobody built the sump gates to keep water out. They were built to let it in, slowly, on purpose.",
    "If you go down, keep to the stone. Wading is slow and the lurkers are not. And when you reach the gates, pull the lever beside the frame and do not wait to see what it lets out."
   ]
  },
  "xul": {
   "title": "What Xul Left",
   "author": "Xul",
   "pages": [
    "I did not die here. I was set here, and told to keep the count of the water, and the water was told to keep the count of me.\n\nHe who comes down with a lamp: you are the first in a long time with a name.",
    "The Lich below is no king. It is the one who was asked to stay when the rest went up the stair, and it stayed too well.\n\nSeal it, or open the sump and let the city drain. Either way, it ends."
   ]
  },
  "vat": {
   "title": "Sap Ledger",
   "author": "A vat-keeper",
   "pages": [
    "Second vat running thin. Third vat sound. Fifth is wept through at the seam; sealed with clay and prayed over.",
    "Hist-sap is not for drinking. Do not let the apprentices say otherwise again."
   ]
  }
 },
